      "side": 1,
      "message": "This function is simply validating the path before stripping its first 2 components so should probably be `(\u0026self, \u0026CStr) -\u003e Result\u003c\u0026CStr\u003e` (or even `-\u003e Option\u003c\u0026CStr\u003e`). Then, the caller can always decide to copy the `\u0026CStr` into its own `CString`. Also, would you mind renaming it to the action it performs on its input (as it doesn\u0027t \"overlay\") e.g. `strip_overlay_(path_)prefix`?\n\nThe implementation could also be made _much_ simpler (easier to review):\n\n1. turn the input `path` into bytes, validate it\u0027s absolute, and split it up:\n\n  ```\n  let chars \u003d path.to_bytes_with_nul();\n  if !chars.starts_with(b\"/\") {\n      return Err(...);\n  }\n  \n  let mut chars_iter \u003d chars[1..].iter();\n  let fragment_len \u003d chars_iter.position(|c| *c \u003d\u003d b\u0027/\u0027).ok_or(...)?;\n  let overlay_len \u003d chars_iter.position(|c| *c \u003d\u003d b\u0027/\u0027).ok_or(...)?;\n  let prefix_len \u003d b\"/\".len() + fragment_len + overlay_len;\n  let (overlay_path, target_path) \u003d chars.split_at(prefix_len + 1);\n  ```\n\n1. validate the `overlay_path` (not sure if this is actually required, TBH)\n\n  ```\n  let (fragment_name, overlay_name) \u003d overlay_path[1..].split_at(fragment_len);\n  let overlay_name \u003d \u0026overlay_name[1..]; // Skip \u0027/\u0027\n  if fragment_name.is_empty() || overlay_name !\u003d b\"__overlay__\" {\n      return Err(...);\n  }\n  ```\n\n1. validate the `__overlay__` node (this could be moved to ctor, BTW)\n\n  ```\n  let overlay_path \u003d CString::new(overlay_path).unwrap();\n  let overlay_node \u003d self.as_fdt().node(\u0026overlay_path)?.ok_or(...)?;\n  let overlay_node_target_path \u003d overlay_node.getprop_str(cstr!(\"target-path\"))?;\n  if overlay_node_target_path !\u003d Some(cstr!(\"/\")) {\n      return Err(...);\n  }\n  ```\n\n1. finally, return the sub-slice of the `CStr` without having to copy anything:\n\n  ```\n  CStr::from_bytes_with_nul(target_path).map_err(...)\n  ```",
