      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Can you at least give it an \"action\" name? The current name is very confusing.\n\nRenamed. PTAL.\n\nPing me if new name is still not-so-good. I\u0027ll be at the office so wouldn\u0027t be bothered by my kids.\n\n-----\n\n\u003e The DT spec implies that having consecutive / ins\u0027t compliant and libfdt probably supports it for backward-compatibility reasons (from the Open Firmware days).\n\nHow does DT spec say exactly? I couldn\u0027t find anything about consecutive `/` from the DT specification pdf. Could you share me\n\nIn the \u0027path\u0027 section in the spec, it doesn\u0027t say anything in detail. It doesn\u0027t even mention about valid deliminator among node names in a path, so I guess that spec says \u0027follows convention\u0027. Consecutive `/` is also allowed in other places, (e.g. `ls` command also accepts it) so I guess that we\u0027d better to support it.\n\n-----\n\n\u003e In particular, these paths are generated by DTC (as opposed to being hand-written) so are very unlikely to contain consecutive /.\n\nI\u0027m bit confused here. If you reply on `DTC` behavior, then you should accept consecutive `/`. If you don\u0027t reply on `DTC` behavior, then we may see consecutive `/` in `__symbols__`. So I decided to support consecutive `/`.\n\nuber-nit; our VM DTBO dtsi has semi-handwritten `__symbols__`. I wrote a `dtsi` with symbol annotation, and then compile -\u003e decompile to get generated `__symbols__` and submitted because I couldn\u0027t change build rule for `dtc -@`. see: b/302093944 for detail.",
